WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.409
Film Courage: I’m sure you are asked about
this scene constantly, but the interrogation

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.700
scene from MENACE II SOCIETY?

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.920
I know you’ve probably been asked this question
so much, but if you can just take us back

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.060
to that day during this scene, what was it
like for you?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:21.270
Bill Duke, Actor/Filmmaker/Author: I’d just
finished doing a film I think it was in Canada

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.980
and acting in a film.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:31.019
I rushed back and at the same day that I came
in, I had to be on the set…the Hughes Brothers

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.800
and I just loved their work.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.800
I liked who they were.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.079
I read the script and I loved it.

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:50.300
It was only one scene and it’s like the
way they set it up got me immediately into

00:00:50.300 --> 00:01:00.570
the mood because the room was dark except
there were three spotlights, one on the young

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:05.339
boy, one on the middle of the table where
the was and once on myself.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:07.540
Everything else was in shadow.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:14.039
And the police had brought this young man
into this place to intimidate him into telling

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:20.649
the truth and so he was sitting there really
nervous, you know?

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:29.869
He thought he was smarter than everybody in
the room and so when I questioned him it was

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:37.529
like I first brought the gun in, put it on
the table and turned it toward him but said

00:01:37.529 --> 00:01:43.289
to him “You are not necessarily getting
out of here alive.

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:46.042
There’s nothing you can do about it.

00:01:46.042 --> 00:01:47.042
What are you going to do?

00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:50.110
We can all say that you reached for the and
we defended ourselves and shot you.”

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.919
We didn’t say those words we just went…[motions
to turning the gun around].

00:01:54.919 --> 00:01:56.029
That was it, that said it.

00:01:56.029 --> 00:02:01.770
I saw that and it was like “Oh boy, okay.”

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:11.150
And I started asking questions about was he
there and he said “No” And he said “You

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:18.402
bought the beer here” and you brought the
beer here.

00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:21.599
And that’s when his hands start shaking.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:28.680
And that’s when I knew that I had him.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:39.239
And 
that little thought they were so smart but

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:41.500
they were so stupid.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:51.359
And it was a feeling of joy when I said to
him “You know something…you know you done

00:02:51.359 --> 00:02:53.530
f*cked up don’t you?”

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.780
“No, no, no…”

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:04.100
“No, no, no, no, no…you know you done
f*cked up right?”

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:13.150
Here’s what’s crazy, I was in Brussels
in Belgium about five or six months ago acting

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.680
in a film with Nicholas Cage called MANDY
right?

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:29.480
I’m sitting in a restaurant eating food
by myself, this young Belgium kid comes up

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:37.120
to me and says “Excuse me sir but you know
you done f*cked up right?”

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.510
I laughed so hard.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:45.409
No matter what country I go to, I’ve made
a lot of films as an actor and director and

00:03:45.409 --> 00:03:50.219
the line that is said to me the most around
the world no matter what city I go to and

00:03:50.219 --> 00:03:55.220
you know something by young kids, that’s
what’s amazing to me.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.220
They’ve all seen that picture.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.340
I don’t know how they see it.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.629
They see it and they all repeat that line.

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:07.170
So yes, it’s interesting.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.840
Film Courage: How has this impacted your life?

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.400
I’m sure it’s got to feel good that they’re
seeing your work.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:21.150
At the same time, maybe you’re surprised
that they are young kids seeing this film

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.710
from all walks of life?

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.530
What does that do to someone?

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:31.800
Bill: Well, it means you did a good job because
you impacted them in that way?

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:37.250
I also wish there was more than they remember
you for than just a line?

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:42.620
You’re appreciate that they’ve seen your
work and appreciate what you’ve done and

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:54.599
that’s always good because I’m very self-critical
of my work and so when someone affirms that

00:04:54.599 --> 00:05:02.260
what I’ve done has impacted them, that’s
very rewarding, that’s very rewarding.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:06.479
Because they don’t have to be kind, they
don’t have to come up to you, they don’t

00:05:06.479 --> 00:05:08.360
have to but they do.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:14.690
At a certain age you’d think they’d forgotten
about you but they haven’t, especially young

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.389
people do it from different countries.

